I’ll first inspect the specimen metadata, pattern, frozen index, and the supplied GSAS-II workflow guidance. Then I’ll identify ordinary phases, refine to physical closure, run the required one-time bounded polish, and export every deliverable from the accepted project.

The specimen is a Cu Kα Bragg–Brentano scan of an 800 °C WO₃/Mn₃O₄ reaction. I’m treating Mn–W–O products and residual precursor/decomposition phases as the ordinary starting inventory; the phase count remains open until coherent residual peak families are exhausted.

A first fingerprint match is decisive for one component: the complete wolframite MnWO₄ reflection family is present, with a common ≈+0.36° displacement. Two strong peaks near 20.3° and 20.9°, plus related weaker peaks, are forbidden/absent in wolframite, so I’m now fitting MnWO₄ as the ordinary phase to isolate that coherent residual family before testing additional structures.

The isolated residual now resolves into two parts. Most peaks at 23.50, 26.94, 29.14, 33.66/34.52, 41.8–42.2°, and their higher-angle continuations match monoclinic γ-WO₃ with the same specimen displacement. I’m adding this ordinary residual precursor first; the distinct 20.32/20.88° family will then be indexed and tested separately.

After modeling γ-WO₃, the remaining peaks form a clean third family: the strong 20.32/20.88/29.14/32.26/35.62/47.02° sequence and many higher-angle members stay positive. It is not hausmannite, bixbyite, MnO, or any WO₃ polymorph in the frozen index. I’m therefore at the instructed missing-reference step: balancing Mn-rich tungstate candidates against residual WO₃ and comparing symmetry-diverse analogue parents on equal footing.

The residual indexes cleanly as rhombohedral Mn₃WO₆: a ≈ 8.8933 Å and c ≈ 10.4528 Å reproduce eleven independent residual reflections with ~0.016° RMS. This also closes the reaction balance exactly as Mn₃WO₆ + 2 WO₃ ↔ 3 MnWO₄. I’m finishing equal cell-level comparisons against the R‑3 Mn₃TeO₆/Cd₃TeO₆ starts and monoclinic/cubic alternatives before choosing the structural basin.

The equal checkpoints strongly select the R‑3 framework: both independent R‑3 parents refine to Rwp ≈7.1% with a stable ~14% contribution, while C2/c, Pa‑3, and P2₁/c candidates converge with essentially zero scale and leave the family unmodeled. Oxygen-coordinate trials reached the ±0.10 Å guardrail in both R‑3 starts, so I’m rolling those sites back and retaining only the interior, reproducible coordinate shifts before profile/specimen refinement.

All coherent peak families are now assigned to MnWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and rhombohedral Mn₃WO₆. I’m moving into the terminal refinement: first testing calibration alternatives, then supported coordinates and common/phase-specific profile terms, with the aim of eliminating the remaining center/wing residuals without introducing correlated or boundary-pinned parameters.

The current model has a fixed 0.08° zero and a refined −522 µm specimen displacement. Because those can trade against lattice metrics, I’m comparing zero-only, displacement-only, and joint calibration branches from the same checkpoint and will retain a correction only if it is interior, converged, and distinguishable in the covariance.

The calibration comparison rejects zero-only (zero pinned at +0.25°) and joint zero/displacement (displacement pinned at −800 µm). The displacement-only branch converged at −656.16(84) µm with zero fixed at 0°, so I’m carrying that physical, well-determined branch forward. Next I’m refining each phase’s heavy and oxygen sites within ±0.10 Å, rolling any bound-hit atom back before joint settling.

The bounded coordinate pass improved Rwp from 7.09% to about 6.40% without short contacts. Most sites stabilize, but the two WO₃ tungsten x coordinates oscillate as an almost redundant pair; that is a correlated alternative, not reliable structural information. I’m completing the diagnostic run, then I’ll roll those tungsten sites back with the already bound-hit oxygen sites and retain only the converged MnWO₄ and Mn₃WO₆ coordinate adjustments.

The accepted coordinate checkpoint now replays cleanly (maximum shift/σ 0.60), settles at Rwp 6.423%, and has no short contacts. I’m now separating peak-shape contributions by alternating two equally developed paths—common instrument broadening first versus phase-specific size/strain first—so a lower Rwp is not accepted at the cost of a pinned or non-identifiable profile parameter.

The common-first profile path is the physical basin: after alternation it is fully interior (U 35.85, W 30.09, X 0.273; all within laboratory ranges) and gives Rwp 3.615%. The phase-first path leaves X pinned at 0.1 and is discarded. Size and microstrain are correlated but remain precisely determined (about 1–6% relative uncertainties), so I’m retaining them while checking the residual for anisotropy, asymmetry, texture, and roughness before adding any nuisance term.

The phase-common isotropic displacement model is strongly supported: it lowers Rwp from 3.615% to 3.363% and refines to 0.00584(10), 0.01052(43), and 0.01182(35) Å² for MnWO₄, WO₃, and Mn₃WO₆, respectively—all interior and converged. I’m now screening the allowed March–Dollase axes phase by phase; this targets the remaining reflection-intensity mismatches without using peak-shape terms to absorb them.

The width-family screen supports uniaxial strain for MnWO₄ along [100] and WO₃ along [001]. The apparently better WO₃ [100] branch is rejected because its equatorial strain pins at 8000 ppm; the [001] branch is converged and interior (6725/9378 ppm). Mn₃WO₆ anisotropy is insignificant. For texture, WO₃ [111] is uniquely favored; Mn₃WO₆’s allowed axes are effectively degenerate, so I’m omitting that inseparable texture term rather than choosing an arbitrary axis.

The nuisance screen keeps only axial-divergence asymmetry, SH/L = 0.02622(49), which is interior and materially improves the fit. Polarization is statistically indistinguishable from the 0.75 checkpoint, while surface roughness pins at a bound and is essentially perfectly correlated with its partner (|r| ≈ 1), so both are rolled back. I’m comparing smooth background orders next, using weighted fit improvement and positivity rather than Rwp alone.

The 10-term smooth background remains preferred by the penalized fit criterion; higher orders shave only ~0.002% Rwp and are not justified. The remaining structured intensity residual is concentrated in the WO₃/Mn₃WO₆ overlap region rather than forming a new peak family. Before terminal settling, I’m checking symmetry-compatible exact WO₃ references from the frozen index to ensure the current low-precision 1960 parent is not forcing nuisance terms to compensate for a poorer atomic model.

The exact-composition check changes the model materially: the transformed neutron WO₃ parent (COD 2311041, in the same P2₁/n cell) lowers Rwp from 3.166% to 2.523% after equal development, with no short contacts. All attempted coordinate shifts were unstable or bound-hit and were rolled back, while its Uiso/profile/texture terms converged physically. I’m accepting that WO₃ basin, removing the inactive older parent, and rebuilding a clean three-phase project before re-screening only the WO₃-dependent profile terms.

With the accepted neutron WO₃ model, the profile terms re-identify cleanly: MnWO₄ remains uniaxial along [100], while WO₃’s supported axis changes to [010] (1981/4411 ppm), well separated from isotropic and other axes. WO₃ texture is small but significant; [110] and [001] are the only close alternatives. I’m now developing a texture-neutral terminal base with the accepted strain axes, then I’ll compare those texture alternatives from that identical checkpoint.

The neutral terminal base reaches Rwp 2.462% with all phase and common width terms interior. One safeguard triggered: SH/L and specimen displacement are now strongly coupled (r = −0.968). Since displacement is independently required by the calibration branches, I’m rolling SH/L back to the fixed 0.02 checkpoint before the final texture comparison, rather than keeping a nominal fit gain from an inseparable profile/calibration pair.

The terminal model now has all three coherent reflection families assigned. I’ve rolled back the correlated axial-divergence refinement and am finishing the last equal-footing texture comparison; after that I’ll lock the physical model, run the mandated one-time bounded polish, and export everything from the accepted project.

The texture test has a unique, weak but statistically supported WO₃ [100] branch (March–Dollase 1.00673(11)); it outperforms every other allowed axis at the same refinement depth. I’m retaining that single term and will now replay the full accepted sequence to verify that the result is stable before the mandated polish.

A simultaneous all-profile trial exposed exactly the failure mode the instructions warn about: the common \(U\) term and Mn₃WO₆ strain ran to tested bounds and became correlated. I stopped that branch. I’m rolling the common \(U\) term back to its last interior checkpoint, then jointly replaying the remaining supported terms; any further bound hit will be removed rather than carried into the polish.

The replay is now stable and physical: \(R_\mathrm{wp}=2.4631\%\), no frozen parameters, no >0.90 correlations, no short contacts, and the two identical terminal replays differ negligibly. The retained common \(U=36.9933\) is interior; all crystallite-size and microstrain terms are also interior, including the narrow WO₃ size term at 0.0444 µm. I’m doing the final residual/geometry audit now, before the one permitted polish invocation.

The residual audit shows only center/wing structure around accepted reflections (the strongest smoothed extrema sit on or between modeled MnWO₄/WO₃/Mn₃WO₆ peaks), not a fourth coherent family. I’ve also confirmed positive displacement parameters, interior laboratory-bounded profile/calibration values, and no crystallographic short contacts. The checkpoint is terminal, so I’m invoking the supplied bounded polish exactly once now.

The bounded polish completed and selected its converged phasewise-coordinate branch: \(R_\mathrm{wp}=1.93115\%\), with stable scale/background/cell settling and unchanged phase-fraction ordering. I’m reviewing the audit’s geometry and bound exclusions now; if it passes, I’ll promote that exact project to `final.gpx` and perform read-only replay/export only.

The audit passes: the selected branch is eligible, has no violations or contacts, its last coordinate pass converged at max shift/esd 0.564, and its final settle converged at 0.0346. Pair minima remain chemically reasonable (e.g. W–O 1.73–1.91 Å, Mn–O 2.08–2.12 Å). I’m accepting that immutable branch and will now only hash-check, replay-check on a copy, and export.

The independent atomic replay succeeded without optimizing any parameter, left `final.gpx` byte-for-byte unchanged, regenerated the full pattern from structure factors, and raised no profile-review flags. Its crystalline fractions are 68.6649 wt% MnWO₄, 15.6734 wt% γ-WO₃, and 15.6617 wt% Mn₃WO₆. I’m now exporting the named CIFs and profile from that exact project and assembling the machine-readable result and concise uncertainty report.

The files themselves are consistent, but the local checker rejected the profile header spelling (`two_theta_deg`) even though the data were valid. I’m changing only that export label to the checker’s recognized `two_theta`; the numerical profile and final project remain untouched.

Completed and replay-verified from the final GSAS-II project.

